#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124331 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124522 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "289ae874-f555-50ee-896f-eb2188004124" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 3x3 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"}] :question/id #uuid "4b2fcf5d-0da3-5676-b4c6-54095c3fb45c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5486" :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda$ of the matrix \n![](images/45/4549ab32dddcf46e297a3238d71a51657bbcf5fa1c4b2659000c023fff6f938d.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿![](images/99/99bf36b6acbdd21ab16ca6b434122fed7c474103f6e88ec5688261d5848d542c.svg)\n\nThe characteristic equation is $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0.$ \n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-9-\\lambda  & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 9-\\lambda  & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )(9-\\lambda )(6-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +9)(\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=-9,$ $\\lambda_2=9,$ and $\\lambda_3=6.$ \n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are always the entries on the main diagonal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124332 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124523 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5abea3ab-76a7-5598-8ede-b4cf88dd47d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues of a General 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "76c52a74-9b98-5df5-ac63-64e92af1a580" :question/math-academy-id "e-5487" :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues ![](images/98/98a445be63a324ba80515c96b7f891797392bdeafb375acb75ce1613f4475d4c.svg) of the matrix \n![](images/f1/f1bc09a8e52ccd286aa0e28fee7f60b17b48ce7423eb897269ab9fda387a93dc.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find 